{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1779119731,"nonce":"BTP5dyPXj9ue2WvVhlU1vdmIlx4=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHOK4fCvM8AAAABCndB38674nsp"},"target":"4c9e411005fd73e766d4e5ab5f58f0a4445aacf437c48aaffeb03d3be0e48170","message":"## Context\n\nThe 04-27 deployment ended with a partial operator-side network failure\n(see [`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md)\n§ \"Op-router forwarding asymmetry\", § \"Recovery: many cycles, root cause indeterminate\"):\n\n- Established TCP/SSH sessions stayed alive all day.\n- **New** connections from operator (deadpool, 192.168.13.x) to mercat / gabby failed.\n- Op-router-sourced ping (192.168.13.1 → mercat) **worked**.\n- Deadpool-sourced ping (192.168.13.x → mercat) **failed**, with ICMP arriving at\n  mercat per `tcpdump` on `br-lan` but no reply visible.\n- Recovery required many power cycles across op router + MikroTik bridge + boat.\n  Root cause not isolated.\n\nThat asymmetry — same destination, different sources, different result — is\nexactly what NAT (specifically the operator-router LAN-zone masquerade we\ndisabled on 2026-04-14) would have collapsed.\n\n## 04-14 masquerade-removal context\n\n`ccomjhc_project11/documentation/bizzyboat_network_debug_2026-04-14.md` disabled\n`masq` on the op-router LAN zone and added static return routes on the bridge\ndevices, deliberately, to surface real source IPs in bridge-device logs. The\ndecision review (`docs/bizzyboat_deployment_log.md:1642-1666`) noted \"no known\nunintended side effects\" — written before the 04-27 event.\n\n## Subsequent observations\n\n- **2026-04-29** ([`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-29_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-29_dev_logs.md)):\n  `ping.bizzy: salmon_direct` flapped 25–70% loss over the WiFi-bridge path\n  while VPN-routed equivalents were 1–5% bad. Candidate causes include\n  op-router NAT path / boat-router masquerade rules; not yet root-caused.\n- **2026-05-01**: bandwidth-saturation problems were the dominant pattern;\n  unrelated to NAT/forwarding plumbing.\n\n## Working hypothesis\n\nRemoving masquerade may have left a return-path / conntrack / zone-classification\ngap that surfaces under sustained load. Re-enabling masquerade would mask the\nsymptom but not the underlying state pathology.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Snapshot first.** Capture config + state from all 4 network devices\n   (boat Teltonika, op Teltonika, OmniTIK, SXTsq). Tracked as a sub-task:\n   `CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#57` (snapshots go to that repo because it\n   contains the private network config).\n2. **Synthesize** against the documented architecture\n   ([`docs/bizzyboat_network.md`](docs/bizzyboat_network.md)) and the 04-14\n   change record. Specifically check:\n   - Op-router LAN-zone masq still `0`, any accreted custom rules,\n     FORWARD-chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24`.\n   - Boat-router FORWARD-chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24`.\n   - Bridge-device return routes still present and persistent post power-cycle.\n   - Conntrack ceilings vs counts under steady state.\n   - Any drift from the documented 04-14 state.\n3. **Decide** based on data:\n   - Fix the underlying forwarding-asymmetry source if identified, OR\n   - Re-enable masquerade as a pragmatic class-prep choice, accepting the\n     diagnostic-IP cost knowingly.\n\n## Related\n\n- Sub-task: `CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#57` (snapshot collection)\n- Related (not a duplicate): #97 (op-side `/diagnostics` recorder)\n- Source incident: [`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md)\n- 04-14 change: `ccomjhc_project11/documentation/bizzyboat_network_debug_2026-04-14.md`\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779125939,"nonce":"ePfY4Z1svEarWzij7mGRfOctOBQ=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCwslrw","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/128#issuecomment-4480247215"},"message":"## Synthesis — 2026-05-18 snapshots vs. masquerade hypothesis\n\nSnapshots collected with all four devices ping-clean from `deadpool` (healthy steady state). Captures live in [CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#58](https://github.com/CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11/pull/58).\n\n### Hypothesis check — every plan-item from the original post\n\n| # | Check | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Op-router LAN-zone masq still `0` | ✅ No `option masq` on `lan` zone; `zone_lan_postrouting` chain is empty (`[0:0]`). WAN/WireGuard/OpenVPN/ZeroTier still masq. |\n| 1b | Any accreted custom rules for LAN? | ✅ None. `firewall.user` only contains the documented NETMAP rules + Strongswan hotplug stubs. |\n| 1c | Op-router FORWARD chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24` | ✅ Packets enter on `br-lan`, hit `zone_lan_forward` → `zone_lan_dest_ACCEPT` (intra-zone, both bridge subnets in `lan` zone). Established traffic short-circuits via `--ctstate RELATED,ESTABLISHED -j ACCEPT` (rule 5). |\n| 2 | Boat-router FORWARD chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24` | ✅ Persistent UCI-configured static route: `192.168.13.0/24 via 172.16.20.2 dev eth0.3`. Return path intact. |\n| 3 | Bridge-device return routes still present | ✅ OmniTIK (`.3`) and SXTsq (`.4`) both have **active static** routes for `192.168.20.0/24 → 172.16.20.1` and `192.168.13.0/24 → 172.16.20.2`. Survives power cycles. |\n| 4 | Conntrack ceilings vs counts | ✅ Op: 917 / 31744 (~3%). Boat: 127 / 31744 (\u003c0.5%). Not even close to the bottleneck. |\n| 5 | Drift from documented 04-14 state | ✅ None observed. Every element of the 04-14 fix is intact. |\n\n### Verdict\n\n**The masquerade-removal hypothesis is refuted by the data.** All static configuration elements of the 04-14 change (LAN-zone masq off, return routes on bridge devices, return route on boat router) are intact and working. Conntrack has 30× headroom over current usage. The deadpool→mercat forwarding asymmetry observed on 04-27 cannot be a return-path bug introduced by 04-14, because the return paths are in fact in place.\n\n### Stronger candidate cause — found in SXTsq log\n\nSXTsq's `/log print` reveals a recurring pattern of wireless instability:\n\n```\n2026-04-14 09:52:22  wireless,info C4:AD:34:90:72:4C@wlan1: lost connection, extensive data loss\n2026-04-27 22:54:23  wireless,info ... established connection on 5745000, SSID bizzy_bridge\n2026-04-28 05:47:42  wireless,info ... lost connection, extensive data loss\n2026-05-01 01:29:21  wireless,info ... established connection\n2026-05-01 02:59:32  wireless,info ... lost connection, received deauth: group key handshake timeout (16)\n2026-05-01 02:59:34  wireless,info ... established connection\n```\n\nThese are **bridge-link drops**, not the same as the WiFi-bridge SNR collapse to 5 dB that was already noted in the 04-29 dev log (that one was on the boat antenna, MAC `08:55:31:E0:74:D4`). The SXTsq log timestamps look offset (SXTsq's clock has no NTP — OmniTIK's clock thinks today is 2026-03-09, boat router thinks it's Apr 21), so I won't claim exact correlation with the 04-27 16:00 EDT failure window — but the pattern of \"lost connection, extensive data loss\" recurring 4+ times in a month is consistent with the kind of intermittent L2 pathology that would explain:\n\n- New TCP connections from deadpool failing while established ones survive (established flows are caught by op-router's RELATED,ESTABLISHED rule before any zone-dispatch logic; new flows depend on the full path being healthy end-to-end)\n- \"Many power cycles to recover\" — wireless association state on RouterOS can wedge after marginal-RF events and only fully clear on reboot\n- Op-router-sourced ping working while deadpool-sourced failed — different L2 source MACs may have been treated differently by a wedged bridge device\n\nAlso worth noting: bridge devices' firewalls are **all disabled** (per the 04-14 / 03-25 decision that they're bridges behind two routers). That includes `fasttrack-connection`, which means there's no fast-path for established flows on the bridge devices — every packet goes through the full slow path. Not a bug, but a relevant detail for recovery dynamics after a state wedge.\n\n### Recommendation\n\n1. **Keep masquerade off.** Re-enabling it would not have prevented the 04-27 incident (return paths weren't the problem) and would cost the diagnostic-IP win we got from 04-14.\n\n2. **Instrument the bridge devices.** SXTsq's `lost connection` log entries are the strongest fresh signal we have. Carry-forward: extend `mikrotik_monitor` (in `ros2_network_monitor`) to surface `wireless,info` log events (or `/interface wireless monitor` stats) so deauth/loss events become a `/diagnostics` signal in real time instead of forensic-only.\n\n3. **Address the bridge-device clock drift while we're in here.** SXTsq/OmniTIK clocks are wildly wrong (no NTP), which makes correlating their wireless log timestamps with field events nearly impossible. A small RouterOS config push to point NTP at the op router (which already runs ntpd) would close that gap. Not blocking, but easy to bundle.\n\n4. **Defer the \"default-gateway instead of per-subnet static routes\" simplification** (mentioned in `bizzyboat_deployment_log.md:1656-1661`). The per-subnet routes are working; cleanup is not urgent.\n\n### Disposition\n\nClosing as **hypothesis refuted, no config change needed**. Will leave this open until @rolker confirms the recommendation set, then we'll spin out a separate issue for #2 (mikrotik_monitor wireless event surfacing) against `ros2_network_monitor`.\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779127004,"nonce":"PLfxhD/d4DzFR7RDBKvPHSYC5qw=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCw1LGw","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/128#issuecomment-4480387867"},"message":"## Follow-up issues filed for the synthesis recommendations\n\n- [`rolker/ros2_network_monitor#21`](https://github.com/rolker/ros2_network_monitor/issues/21) — `mikrotik_monitor`: surface wireless events (associations, drops, signal) as **observable** diagnostics (no auto-elevated severity — drops are routine for marine ops).\n- [`CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#59`](https://github.com/CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11/issues/59) — Bridge-device NTP: OmniTIK → TM2000B (192.168.20.123) + boat router (172.16.20.1) as secondary; SXTsq → op router (172.16.20.2).\n\nRecommendation #1 (keep masquerade off) needs no action. Recommendation #4 (default-gateway route simplification) remains deferred.\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779129740,"nonce":"gZimnctsR3N9UWxs2jz3Lrm+304=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCxOsLA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/128#issuecomment-4480805932"},"message":"## Update — issue #59 re-scoped\n\nWhile drafting the bridge-NTP rollout, discovered that **neither RUTX11 router actually serves NTP today.** Boat router's clock is ~4 weeks behind real time (the symptom that started this); op router's clock is fine but only because it runs `ntp_gps + gps_sync='1'` to keep its own local clock from GNSS — it does *not* serve.\n\nMechanism:\n\n- Memory `project_ntp_rutx11.md` (2026-04-10) claimed *\"Both RUTX11 routers run ISC ntpd 4.2.8p15\"* with `/etc/ntp.conf` + `file_flag='1'` in UCI to make `untpd` read the file.\n- Current `opkg list-installed` on **both routers**: no `ntp` package. Only `untpd`, `ntpclient`, `ntp_gps`. The UCI `file_flag='1'` config is still there but unread (nothing parses `package ntpd` namespace).\n- Boat router logread captured: `1045 Tue Apr 21 22:07:15 2026 system.notice Firmware update to RUTX_R_00.07.22.1 is now available` — there's been firmware activity on/around 04-21. Teltonika firmware updates wipe `/overlay`, where opkg-installed packages live, while preserving `/etc/config`. That matches what we observe: custom UCI survived, ISC ntpd package didn't.\n\n**Decision:** drop the routers from the bridge-NTP plan. Bridge devices now point only at TM2000B + a chrony peer:\n\n- OmniTIK: TM2000B (192.168.20.123) + gabby (192.168.20.5)\n- SXTsq: TM2000B (192.168.20.123) + salmon (192.168.13.142)\n\nBoth directly reachable via existing static routes. No new router work needed for the bridge-NTP rollout. See CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#59 for the revised plan.\n\n**Separate follow-on (not in scope here):** restoring ISC ntpd on the routers and adding a firmware-survival mechanism would be its own piece of work. Tracking that in `project_ntp_rutx11.md` until/unless it becomes operationally needed — bridge devices don't require it.\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779139677,"nonce":"C83zz3/zwqO/rd6h9lOocIUDByU=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCyvT4Q","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/128#issuecomment-4482388961"},"message":"## Correction: TM2000B failure is at cold-boot, not mid-operation — likely an MMC-failure compounding effect\n\nUpdating the [earlier TM2000B failure-mode comment](#issuecomment-4480805932) with a corrected diagnosis. Two key facts that the earlier read missed:\n\n1. **TM2000B is not powered when the boat is off.** It cold-boots whenever the boat is powered up.\n2. The log shows **only one boot** (Apr 10 14:08:00) — even though the boat has been powered up multiple times since Apr 10. So either the log captures only the most-recent boot, or all boots since Apr 10 stamp themselves with the same Apr-10 time — and the latter is more consistent with the visible `MMC FAILURE WITH CARD DETECT` log line at boot.\n\n### Hypothesis: MMC failure → frozen sysfixtime → multi-week clock jump at GPS lock → firmware bug\n\nTM2000B has no battery-backed RTC. The OS uses `sysfixtime` to persist the system clock across reboots: on shutdown (or periodically) it writes a timestamp to flash; on boot it reads that file and seeds the clock. With **`MMC FAILURE WITH CARD DETECT` firing at every boot** (visible in the log), persistent writes likely aren't landing — so the sysfixtime stamp stays frozen at whatever was last successfully written, presumably Apr 10.\n\nThat means every cold boot:\n1. System clock initializes to \"Apr 10 14:08:00\" (frozen sysfixtime)\n2. ~73 s later, GPS achieves first 3D lock\n3. Clock jumps forward by however-many days/weeks to actual UTC\n4. **TimeMachines' kernel-side time discipline mishandles the multi-week step** — `tai_offset_stored` resets to 0, `jump_out` fires, the leap-offset state corrupts\n5. Kernel free-runs with `Freq Adjust = 0`, emits alert-priority `Clock IRQ` once per second forever\n6. Appliance stops serving NTP; only ICMP-responsive until power-cycled\n\nPower-cycling fixes it because the *second* boot finds a sysfixtime stamp that's much closer to real time (from the first failed boot's runtime writes, if any landed), so the GPS-step is smaller and the firmware handles it.\n\n### What we know vs. what we don't\n\n| Confirmed | Open |\n|---|---|\n| `MMC FAILURE WITH CARD DETECT` fires at boot | Whether the MMC is fully dead or intermittently failing |\n| `tai_offset_stored=0 jump_out` precedes the wedged state | Whether the bug fires on every boot or only when the clock-jump exceeds some threshold |\n| Power-cycle recovers | Whether sysfixtime is the actual mechanism or something adjacent |\n| Polling-based detection can't prevent it — wedged before first poll cycle | Whether the TM2000B web UI's status panel surfaces MMC health |\n\n### Operational fix (independent of root cause)\n\nEnable **Peer NTP** on the TM2000B web UI, pointing at a reachable network NTP source. Per TimeMachines docs, peer NTP lets the appliance set its system clock from a network source before GPS lock arrives — so when GPS lock does land, the correction step is small and the firmware doesn't trip the bug.\n\nCandidate peer-NTP targets:\n- **gabby** (`192.168.20.5`) — chrony server on the boat LAN. Reliable when gabby is up before TM2000B (boot order matters).\n- **Public pool** (`pool.ntp.org`) — works whenever boat WAN is reachable. Requires TM2000B to have a default route.\n- Both, for redundancy.\n\nThis is the right fix to apply regardless of whether the MMC hypothesis turns out to be correct — it sidesteps the multi-week-clock-jump trigger entirely.\n\n### Verification to confirm the hypothesis\n\nNext time the boat is cold-powered:\n1. As soon as TM2000B is reachable, check its web UI for any storage / system-health indicator. Note exact firmware version.\n2. Pull `/var/log/messages` (and / or `/var/log/dmesg`) immediately — within first 5 min of boot. Compare boot sequence against today's log. If `MMC FAILURE WITH CARD DETECT` is still firing, MMC theory holds.\n3. After GPS lock acquires (~ 1 min from boot), `ntpdate -q time.bizzy.p11.lan` from gabby. If it returns a valid response, today's failure may have been a one-off and the bug isn't every-boot. If it times out, the bug is reproducing → MMC-frozen-sysfixtime theory strengthens.\n\n### Implications for the rest of the time-config rollout\n\nUnchanged from my prior comment:\n\n- **#59 (bridge device NTP)**: TM2000B's reliability is no longer assumed. Bridge devices' fallback chain (TM2000B + chrony peer + public pool) handles this gracefully today.\n- **#129 (chrony cleanup)**: independent of TM2000B's state.\n- **`rolker/snmp_diag#1`**: polling won't *prevent* the wedge, but the trap-receiver follow-on would let us catch GPS-lock-change events at boot.\n\n### Vendor follow-up\n\nThis is now a sharp bug report for TimeMachines: cold boot + stale sysfixtime + multi-week forward GPS-step → leap-offset corruption + persistent `Clock IRQ` storm + NTP service silent. Plus the `MMC FAILURE WITH CARD DETECT` which may be a hardware issue worth their attention.\n\n### Credit\n\nDiagnosis was wrong twice — first read missed that the boat was off (TM2000B not running continuously); second read had the boat power timeline wrong. @rolker had to correct both. The current read is the third pass and is bounded by what the snapshot can actually prove.\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}